<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang={assigns[:site_lang] || "en"} {assigns[:theme_attrs] || %{}} class="scroll-smooth">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <meta name="csrf-token" content={get_csrf_token()} />
    <meta name="description" content="Read-only, searchable mirror of Confluence." />
    <meta name="color-scheme" content="light dark" />
    <.live_title default="Confluence Mirror" suffix=" · Confluence Mirror" phx-no-format>
      {assigns[:page_title]}
    </.live_title>
    <%!-- Written by scripts/favicon.sh; the name is the browser's own default,
    so a shortcut saved without a link still finds it.

    A literal path, not `~p`: with a cache manifest configured, the route
    helper hands back the digested name (`/favicon-e496…ico?vsn=d`), and
    `Plug.Static` serves a top-level file only under the exact name its
    `static_paths/0` allows. `/assets/…` and `/images/…` survive that because
    their first segment is an allowed directory; a file at the root has only
    itself, so the digested icon 404s and the tab falls back to a blank page
    icon. Dev has no manifest, which is why this only ever breaks in a release. --%>
    <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.ico" type="image/x-icon" />
    <link phx-track-static rel="stylesheet" href={~p"/assets/css/app.css"} />
    <%!-- Swagger UI is a bundle of its own and only /api/docs/swagger asks for it:
    the reader pages must not download a vendor UI. --%>
    <link
      :if={assigns[:swagger_ui]}
      phx-track-static
      rel="stylesheet"
      href={~p"/assets/js/swagger.css"}
    />
    <.skin_style css={assigns[:skin_css]} />
    <script defer phx-track-static type="text/javascript" src={~p"/assets/js/app.js"}>
    </script>
    <script
      :if={assigns[:swagger_ui]}
      defer
      phx-track-static
      type="text/javascript"
      src={~p"/assets/js/swagger.js"}
    >
    </script>
  </head>
  <body class="min-h-screen bg-base-100 text-base-content antialiased">
    {@inner_content}
  </body>
</html>
